Subject: fix NULL dereference removing an xattr stored in the inode body

ext4_xattr_remove() searches the inode body first. When the attribute is found
there, use_block stays false and block_finder.s is never populated -- only
block_finder.i was assigned. The non-block branch nonetheless passed
&block_finder.s to ext4_xattr_set_entry(), whose first act is to walk the entry
list starting at s->first. That pointer is NULL, so EXT4_XATTR_IS_LAST_ENTRY()
dereferences address 0 and the process dies with SIGSEGV.

ext4_xattr_set() gets this right in the equivalent branch: it passes
&ibody_finder.s. This makes remove match.

Reproduces with any attribute small enough to live in the inode body, which is
the common case -- mke2fs defaults to 256-byte inodes:

    setxattr /file user.colour blue
    removexattr /file user.colour      -> SIGSEGV

Found with -fsanitize=address,undefined:

    ext4_xattr.c:327:10: runtime error: load of null pointer of type 'uint32_t'
    #0 ext4_xattr_set_entry ext4_xattr.c:327
    #1 ext4_xattr_remove    ext4_xattr.c:1264

diff --git a/src/ext4_xattr.c b/src/ext4_xattr.c
index f8a5778..84687f9 100644
--- a/src/ext4_xattr.c
+++ b/src/ext4_xattr.c
@@ -1260,8 +1260,8 @@ int ext4_xattr_remove(struct ext4_inode_ref *inode_ref, uint8_t name_index,
 		}
 
 	} else {
-		/* Now remove the entry */
-		ext4_xattr_set_entry(&i, &block_finder.s, false);
+		/* Now remove the entry from the inode body */
+		ext4_xattr_set_entry(&i, &ibody_finder.s, false);
 		inode_ref->dirty = true;
 	}
 out:
